#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138282 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138558 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3108f0ab-17b1-5241-93d1-97658cf22601" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Three Collinear Points"}] :question/id #uuid "5b1ee9ef-da39-5f7a-abb5-98149a765b1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-2565" :question/problem "Let $C,$ $D$ and $E$ be three collinear points such that $D$ is between $C$ and $E.$ If $CE = 8\\,\\text{cm}$ and $CD = 6\\,\\text{cm}$, find the length of $\\overline{DE}$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/30/304a0de1777efb414a2504e22d53c1d15a5a0299196a7a9103a02491ff12e4c6.png)\n\nThe segment addition postulate states that\n\n$$\nCE = CD + DE.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values into the formula above, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CE & =CD+DE \\\\ 8 & =6+DE \\\\ 8-6 & =DE \\\\ 2 & =DE\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $DE = 2 \\, \\text{cm}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138283 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138559 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7679cf4-427a-59ed-8b0f-d483af20ede8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between More Than Three Collinear Points"}] :question/id #uuid "cabd7daa-4f22-56ca-8b26-0791857e8eae" :question/math-academy-id "e-2566" :question/problem "Given the diagram shown below, what is the measure of $\\overline{BC}?$\n\n![](images/54/543c7c26c7d56d8ff10f4ece71b74bb5f04edf59fddb6f2c0df549848000abaf.png)" :question/worked-solution "The segment addition postulate can be applied to a set of more than three collinear points. In the given situation, it states that\n\n$$\nAD = AB + BC +CD.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values into the equation above, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AD & =AB+BC+CD \\\\ 21 & =5+BC+12 \\\\ 21 & =17+BC \\\\ 21-17 & =BC \\\\ BC & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138284 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138560 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c48d15-4749-5c47-bc97-a5584216cf2e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of an Unknown Quantity Using Collinearity"}] :question/id #uuid "6df54266-d6d6-54f0-8b61-4b5eabf0fcbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-2567" :question/problem "Given the diagram shown below, what is the value of $x?$\n\n![](images/3f/3fe1e3d1e6f89af1050d0437379a811eabb19212be36f0fecd91ddfaca2594ee.png)" :question/worked-solution "By the segment addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nAD = AB + BC +CD.\n$$\n\nTo solve for $x,$ we can substitute the given information\n\n$$\nAD=30, \\quad AB=x+5, \\quad BC=13, \\quad CD=5x,\n$$\n\n \n\nand then solve the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AD & =AB+BC+CD \\\\ 30 & =(x+5)+13+5x \\\\ 30 & =6x+18 \\\\ 6x & =12 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138285 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138561 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da4a8391-42b6-510c-8cb4-acbbbe7d1585" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of an Unknown Quantity Using Collinearity and Midpoints"}] :question/id #uuid "fc35d08c-518a-5f03-8e5f-627d58c572d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-2568" :question/problem "In the diagram below, the length of the line segment $\\overline{AD}$ is $8d+12,$ $AB = 4d+4,$ and $C$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{BD}.$ Express the length of $\\overline{AC}$ in terms of $d.$\n\n![](images/46/468fd25032d1a36e1658331053a33652cef7ff9ce3d9c95b729d6a7b7ebf1672.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, note that according to the segment addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nAD = AB + BD,\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BD & =AD-AB \\\\  & =(8d+12)-(4d+4) \\\\  & =4d+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $C$ is the midpoint of $BD$, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\frac{1}{2}BD \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4d+8) \\\\  & =2d+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =AB+BC \\\\  & =(4d+4)+(2d+4) \\\\  & =6d+8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496283 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138559 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7679cf4-427a-59ed-8b0f-d483af20ede8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between More Than Three Collinear Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0673e2ce-64c9-54ab-92be-596aa8c61368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495528} {:answer/id #uuid "1dcc7d71-0d06-530d-8640-754ddf2dbe36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495573} {:answer/id #uuid "3388ca0c-124f-5d4f-985f-3ed9da4c4b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495630} {:answer/id #uuid "65eec781-812e-52cb-8c3d-3be3f6a25654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495737} {:answer/id #uuid "8dfff003-e842-5741-8e64-38def0061011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0673e2ce-64c9-54ab-92be-596aa8c61368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495528} :answer-field/id #uuid "732b3c8c-f12e-5758-98b7-59669894ad91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8d7c6a20-2db9-5df2-9d3a-9d9b2f89a1a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-114224" :question/problem "![](images/1e/1e151e2518ad2297f86aa25ebdd395ac62c2d05f9e6781d0d82e66a10418aa5a.png)\n\nGiven the diagram shown above, what is the measure of $\\overset{―}{CD}?$" :question/worked-solution "The segments addition postulate can be applied to a set of more than three collinear points. In the given situation, it states that \n\n$$\nAD=AB+BC+CD.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values into the equation above, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AD & =AB+BC+CD \\\\ 54 & =17+22+CD \\\\ 54 & =39+CD \\\\ 54-39 & =CD \\\\ CD & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138560 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c48d15-4749-5c47-bc97-a5584216cf2e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of an Unknown Quantity Using Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e3c00a1-2f50-5f98-846b-52df39aef64a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495575} {:answer/id #uuid "2077f56d-45f8-5b3a-b158-dc49085ead2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495579} {:answer/id #uuid "380ed133-1860-5fee-99ea-3438d7d1c75b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495643} {:answer/id #uuid "55ebe43f-ac41-5bfb-b1c4-f25e69da581b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495705} {:answer/id #uuid "d1abf0bf-f1db-5257-9b0e-796cd5006fd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e3c00a1-2f50-5f98-846b-52df39aef64a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495575} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5173e51-80b0-5079-933a-f2cad1712477" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d31182c-3033-5d05-9db8-c0bd99386616" :question/math-academy-id "q-114236" :question/problem "![](images/d2/d2e722f9b566c903edaae9c90e2f0cac4f6b1a55e74b0c31724a2f675e5d16f5.png)\n\nGiven the diagram shown above, what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "By the segment addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nAD=AB+BC+CD.\n$$\n\nTo solve for $x,$ we can substitute the given information\n\n$$\nAD=69,\\,AB=5x-2,\\,BC=x+2,\\,CD=27,\n$$\n\n  \n\nand then solve the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AD & =AB+BC+CD \\\\ 69 & =(5x-2)+(x+2)+27 \\\\ 69 & =6x+27 \\\\ 6x & =42 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138558 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3108f0ab-17b1-5241-93d1-97658cf22601" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Three Collinear Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e092e81-1519-5157-892b-7206cf8ecf7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495544} {:answer/id #uuid "1cb27e54-c3a6-54a4-81e6-dff4e2cfb317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495567} {:answer/id #uuid "466d6286-724d-5d86-ba8f-fa6e2c735aaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495670} {:answer/id #uuid "978bb03f-1aca-511f-aa41-17c7b150bd8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495849} {:answer/id #uuid "db3f6572-bf17-549c-9b8b-8c0a3cb923e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e092e81-1519-5157-892b-7206cf8ecf7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495544} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1cdf107-7e54-5936-9f13-a265112a8b8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1734fbf3-8c46-59f6-b44b-50223df284ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-25786" :question/problem "Let $P$, $Q$ and $R$ be three collinear points such that $PQ=11\\text{ cm}$ and $QR=16\\text{ cm}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P$ **cannot** lie between $Q$ and $R$\n\nII. $Q$ **cannot** lie between $P$ and $R$\n\nIII. $R$ **cannot** lie between $P$ and $Q$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement.\n\nIf $P$ is between $Q$ and $R$, then, by the segments addition postulate, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QR & =PQ+PR \\\\ 16 & =11+PR \\\\ 16-11 & =PR \\\\ PR & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/10/10a73ef77e6f4367b971465ab6be0a4decdb8a36a2931944fbe10be11c92475f.png)\n\nTherefore, $P$ can lie between $Q$ and $R,$ so statement I is false.\n\nIf $Q$ is between $P$ and $R$, then again, by the segments addition postulate, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PR & =PQ+QR \\\\  & =11+16 \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/7b/7b8d7c69c2a8d20e3c9a7e06a5cc5d3d456b117f4e7d7ce708cacf27fece0d68.png)\n\nTherefore, $Q$ can lie between $P$ and $R,$ so statement II is false.\n\nIf $R$ is between $P$ and $Q$, then, by the segments addition postulate, we would have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =PR+QR \\\\ 11 & =PR+16 \\\\ PR & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis can't happen since the length of a segment can't be negative. \n\nTherefore, $R$ cannot lie between $P$ and $Q,$ so statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138561 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da4a8391-42b6-510c-8cb4-acbbbe7d1585" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of an Unknown Quantity Using Collinearity and Midpoints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d849ff7-d474-5285-87a4-e389ead36b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3d+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495615} {:answer/id #uuid "7f1faa59-ed63-5247-bf8e-b08d97204ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6d+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495783} {:answer/id #uuid "9047f0a9-eeb4-5cec-89a7-53f6ecb81214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6d+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495828} {:answer/id #uuid "e66f03da-3166-5031-8256-01caa0f80b9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3d+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496019} {:answer/id #uuid "fc2fb2f6-10e5-5b50-ab79-52fbfef7fbfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2d+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e66f03da-3166-5031-8256-01caa0f80b9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3d+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496019} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9283bb4-6ecf-5de6-856e-2ac2b30d7426" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01a9d857-28c1-509b-bab9-73d8c8e051ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-52726" :question/problem "![](images/46/468fd25032d1a36e1658331053a33652cef7ff9ce3d9c95b729d6a7b7ebf1672.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the length of the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is $5d+6,$ and $AD=11d+12.$ If $C$ is the midpoint of $\\overset{―}{BD},$ find the length of $\\overset{―}{CD}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that according to the segment addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nAD=AB+BD,\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BD & =AD-AB \\\\  & =(11d+12)-(5d+6) \\\\  & =6d+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $C$ is the midpoint of $BD$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =\\frac{1}{2}BD \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(6d+6) \\\\  & =3d+3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138561 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da4a8391-42b6-510c-8cb4-acbbbe7d1585" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of an Unknown Quantity Using Collinearity and Midpoints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34f63758-cb7f-51c4-b50a-99c4f9fc851b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3d+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495635} {:answer/id #uuid "3788d627-79f5-52f8-89b8-4c1f3e69bb8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4d+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495641} {:answer/id #uuid "72489cd2-014c-5ed0-9f67-4d47f96fe571" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5d+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495761} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb3529a-7655-57cc-b0dd-1c6b0e08d11f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4d+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495858} {:answer/id #uuid "cd5f1430-ed4b-5ddd-affd-bd4db6a30b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3d+14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3788d627-79f5-52f8-89b8-4c1f3e69bb8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4d+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495641} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e07eac4-8d4b-5854-aa03-15bb429fea2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec2f525c-530a-5760-a56d-781b9e8e9599" :question/math-academy-id "q-52737" :question/problem "![](images/46/468fd25032d1a36e1658331053a33652cef7ff9ce3d9c95b729d6a7b7ebf1672.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the length of $\\overset{―}{AD}$ is $5d+12,$ and $AB=3d+2.$ If $C$ is the midpoint of $\\overset{―}{BD},$ find the length of $\\overset{―}{AC}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that according to the segment addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nAD=AB+BD,\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BD & =AD-AB \\\\  & =(5d+12)-(3d+2) \\\\  & =2d+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $C$ is the midpoint of $\\overset{―}{BD},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =AB+\\frac{1}{2}BD \\\\  & =3d+2+\\frac{1}{2}(2d+10) \\\\  & =3d+2+d+5 \\\\  & =4d+7\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138560 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c48d15-4749-5c47-bc97-a5584216cf2e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of an Unknown Quantity Using Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fb0c933-996e-5d1c-9ef3-4fef3078f7fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495656} {:answer/id #uuid "4cfde83d-10b7-55f7-b994-0496ffaafade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495687} {:answer/id #uuid "c33467e9-57e7-5c90-afed-9d4db460f4a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495937} {:answer/id #uuid "d406d6d6-8c33-5f59-b805-134acba76c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495976} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9390d3-6487-55df-8143-0b962be456f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cfde83d-10b7-55f7-b994-0496ffaafade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495687} :answer-field/id #uuid "75eee19d-3154-5c95-b3ac-781e034cbd00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce45e183-4121-5c7a-893b-6d26e45f745c" :question/math-academy-id "q-67596" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a400fb78a04d7a097f59b1ce38919362b64a37660b34313584c58c03547c6785.png)\n\nGiven the diagram shown above, what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "By the segment addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nAD=AB+BC+CD.\n$$\n\nTo solve for $x,$ we can substitute the given information\n\n$$\nAD=33,\\,AB=4x,\\,BC=16-x,\\,CD=8,\n$$\n\n   \n\nand then solve the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AD & =AB+BC+CD \\\\ 33 & =4x+(16-x)+8 \\\\ 33 & =3x+24 \\\\ 9x & =3 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496315 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138558 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3108f0ab-17b1-5241-93d1-97658cf22601" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Three Collinear Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "256972b2-9a8a-5711-9700-39e15d302c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495592} {:answer/id #uuid "2d752876-29d0-538c-b43b-c0eca6b040a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495614} {:answer/id #uuid "6fcb0ff8-cf1e-5af3-911b-4cded950d7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495755} {:answer/id #uuid "ae3a56e8-fb91-53c7-b022-e3a8f6b2db0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495902} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e3b7f4-fcf4-590a-87b6-7a75fdf58038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "256972b2-9a8a-5711-9700-39e15d302c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495592} :answer-field/id #uuid "43d96bdb-5516-5f1b-b865-c20f14f73879" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "da73dbf4-60d5-5996-a49e-cd36cf80dd15" :question/math-academy-id "q-72037" :question/problem "Let $P$, $Q$ and $R$ be three collinear points such that $PQ=7\\text{ cm}$ and $QR=13\\text{ cm}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P$ **cannot** lie between $Q$ and $R$\n\nII. $Q$ **cannot** lie between $P$ and $R$\n\nIII. $R$ **cannot** lie between $P$ and $Q$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement.\n\nIf $P$ is between $Q$ and $R$, then, by the segments addition postulate, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QR & =PQ+PR \\\\ 13 & =7+PR \\\\ 13-7 & =PR \\\\ PR & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/d7/d7396a24e0e8884195184a4fca2b4e0fd9cfc2462249c7dc3a0c66901d062c5f.png)\n\nTherefore, $P$ can lie between $Q$ and $R,$ so statement I is false.\n\nIf $Q$ is between $P$ and $R$, then again, by the segments addition postulate, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PR & =PQ+QR \\\\  & =7+13 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/2d/2dc899f0b5d08bf4a5b5e1833e62e4a3290c8da83dbd376ed51d185c1ed08e94.png)\n\nTherefore, $Q$ can lie between $P$ and $R,$ so statement II is false.\n\nIf $R$ is between $P$ and $Q$, then, by the segments addition postulate, we would have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =PR+QR \\\\ 7 & =PR+13 \\\\ PR & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis can't happen since the length of a segment can't be negative. \n\nTherefore, $R$ cannot lie between $P$ and $Q,$ so statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138559 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7679cf4-427a-59ed-8b0f-d483af20ede8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between More Than Three Collinear Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a5ce289-87a3-5a09-a506-c6f500d39cbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495647} {:answer/id #uuid "57d741f4-5570-5294-a7f3-0dc358306cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495710} {:answer/id #uuid "62887a28-853e-5233-bc37-5b59c6cf567d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495730} {:answer/id #uuid "7acc59f4-8494-583b-8bc4-7a1c4912842f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495774} {:answer/id #uuid "a2e01d48-bcaf-53e4-8024-07ec28abd08c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2e01d48-bcaf-53e4-8024-07ec28abd08c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495874} :answer-field/id #uuid "55521a7c-750c-515c-af51-bff169578548" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "64c26ddd-268e-5f64-a3fd-ce3f88c6ef7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-72040" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b0a3fc0be34d5a9d94d1f0d4e8a1b30449a5a23bd0401b079156404fe91c043f.png)\n\nGiven the diagram shown above, what is the measure of $\\overset{―}{AB}?$" :question/worked-solution "The segments addition postulate can be applied to a set of more than three collinear points. In the given situation, it states that\n\n$$\nAD=AB+BC+CD.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values into the equation above, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AD & =AB+BC+CD \\\\ 46 & =AB+25+11 \\\\ 46 & =AB+36 \\\\ 46-36 & =AB \\\\ AB & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
